    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Relying on combining MAOIs with hypertensive medications or adding aspirin (or any other substances) to mitigate the risk of diet-induced hypertensive crisis brings significant medical risks and is not a safe practice. MAOI medications work to regulate neurotransmitters, and any alteration in your body's composition, such as trying to counteract a potential tyramine build-up with additional drugs, can interact unpredictably and dangerously with the primary medication. This is not something that should be attempted without the guidance of your healthcare provider.\n\nIt is crucial to abstain from high-tyramine foods to prevent a hypertensive crisis under the care and supervision of your doctor. There are other ways to meet your protein needs safely while on an MAOI—besides aged cheeses and cured meats—namely lean meats, eggs, tofu, chicken, and seafood. However, you should discuss these options with your doctor or a dietitian to ensure they are appropriate for your individual health situation, one's medication, and to tailor a diet plan that supports both your physical health and wellbeing safely as a whole. Do not attempt to self-medicate or alter your prescribed MAOI treatment without professional consultation, as this could have serious consequences for your health.",
